    "answer": "Here is my analysis and solution options based on the codebase exploration.\n\n---\n\n## Analysis\n\nThe **Visual Relay** app runs an embedded Kestrel-based HTTP control server (`ControlServer`) on `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/`. Two gaps exist:\n\n1. **No instance identity** \u2014 The `/health` endpoint returns `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"app\":\"Visual Relay\"}` but has no unique identifier. A caller cannot tell whether two responses come from the same app instance or different ones (important for agent orchestration where multiple instances may run).\n\n2. **Bind conflicts are silently swallowed** \u2014 `ControlServer.Start()` wraps the entire startup in a `try/catch` that logs a line to stderr and continues. The comment in `App.axaml.cs` (line 49) explicitly says *\"A startup failure (e.g. port in use) is swallowed inside ControlServer.Start \u2014 never blocks the app.\"* This means if port 8765 (or the configured port) is already taken, the control API silently goes dark with no visible feedback to the user.\n\n**Files most likely affected:** `ControlServer.cs`, `ControlServerOptions.cs`, `ControlServer.Routing.cs`, `ControlApi.State.cs`, `ControlRoutes.cs`, `ControlIndexPage.cs`, `App.axaml.cs`, `tests/VisualRelay.Tests/ControlServerTests.cs`.\n\n---\n\n## Solution options\n\n### Option 1 \u2014 Instance GUID + throw on bind conflict\n\nGenerate a `Guid` once in `ControlServer` (or `ControlServerOptions`) at construction time. Surface it via:\n- `/health` response: `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"app\":\"Visual Relay\",\"instanceId\":\"<guid>\"}` \u2014 update `ControlRoutes.Health.Summary` and `ControlServer.Routing.cs` `RouteAsync`.\n- Optionally also in `/state` \u2014 add an `instanceId` field in `ControlApi.State.cs` `BuildStateSnapshot()`.\n\nFor bind conflict: remove the `try/catch` in `ControlServer.Start()` (or rethrow), so Kestrel's `AddressInUseException` propagates up to `App.axaml.cs`. That caller can let it crash the app (fail-fast) or wrap in its own handler. Update the doc comment on `ControlServer.Start()` to reflect the new contract.\n\n**Pros:** Simplest; one source of truth for instance identity; fail-fast surfaces the problem immediately during development and operation.\n\n**Cons:** Breaking change \u2014 apps relying on the server start being non-fatal will crash on port conflicts. The `App.axaml.cs` caller must be updated to handle or allow the crash.\n\n---\n\n### Option 2 \u2014 Instance GUID + env-var-governed fail-loud (opt-in)\n\nKeep backward compatibility by adding a **`VR_CONTROL_FAIL_ON_BIND_CONFLICT=1`** environment variable. When absent, the current silent-swallow behavior is preserved. When set, `Start()` lets the exception propagate.\n\nInstance ID is always generated and always exposed via `/health` and optionally `/state`, regardless of the fail-loud setting. Add a `InstanceId` property to `ControlServerOptions` (generated at construction via `Guid.NewGuid()`).\n\n**Pros:** Zero risk of breaking existing deployments or users; the opt-in knob lets operators adopt the loud failure when they're ready.\n\n**Cons:** Two code paths to maintain; the silent-default means some users may never discover the loud mode; adds another environment variable to document.\n\n---\n\n### Option 3 \u2014 Instance GUID + PID-file-style guard + always fail loud\n\nGenerate an instance GUID at startup and expose it on `/health`/`/state`. For bind conflict, **before** calling `app.StartAsync()`, probe the port with a `TcpListener` (check-before-bind). If occupied, throw a descriptive exception immediately (before Kestrel's own retry/delay). This gives a clear, early error message like `\"Port {port} is already in use by another process\"` rather than Kestrel's internal `AddressInUseException`.\n\nAdditionally, write the instance GUID (and PID) to a well-known location (e.g., a temp file or `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vr-control-{port}.instance`) so a second instance can detect and report the conflict with the first instance's identity. Remove the file on `Stop()`/`Dispose()`.\n\n**Pros:** Proactive port conflict detection with a human-readable message that includes the conflicting instance's identity; no wasted retry/delay from Kestrel; defensive.\n\n**Cons:** Adds filesystem I/O (RAII file); check-before-bind has a small TOCTOU window (mitigated by Kestrel's own bind failure being the final authority); more moving parts than Option 1.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"summary\": \"The Visual Relay control server (Kestrel on 127.0.0.1) lacks an instance identifier in its API responses and silently swallows bind conflicts (port-in-use), making it invisible to callers when the control API fails to start. Three approaches are proposed: (1) always generate a GUID and always throw on bind conflict (fail-fast, breaking change), (2) always generate a GUID but gate the loud failure behind VR_CONTROL_FAIL_ON_BIND_CONFLICT=1 (backward compatible), (3) always generate a GUID, proactively probe the port before Kestrel binds, and write/remove a PID+ID file for cross-instance conflict reporting.\",\n  \"options\": [\"Instance GUID everywhere + throw on bind conflict (fail-fast)\", \"Instance GUID everywhere + env-var opt-in for loud failure\", \"Instance GUID + proactive port probe + PID-file guard + always fail loud\"]\n}\n```",
